.SS "int32_t schultz_slider_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, uint32_t orientation, float minimum, float maximum, float value, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a slider: a value dragged along a track\&. Pressing or dragging anywhere on it moves the thumb there\&. Left and down step back, right and up step forward, home and end jump to the ends\&. Those keys are consumed, so an arrow that moved a slider does not also move focus\&. The value starts at \fCvalue\fP and is always inside the range\&.
.PP
The track is drawn in \fCborder\&.color\fP, the part behind the thumb in \fCbackground\fP, and the thumb in \fCselection\&.color\fP\&.
.PP
A vertical slider counts upward: the minimum is at the bottom, which is the way a person reads a column\&. Up and right still step forward and down and left still step back, so the keys mean the same thing either way round\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIorientation\fP SCHULTZ_ORIENT_HORIZONTAL or _VERTICAL\&. 
.br
\fIminimum\fP Lowest value\&. 
.br
\fImaximum\fP Highest value\&. Must not be below the minimum\&. 
.br
\fIvalue\fP Starting value, clamped into the range\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when the range is backwards or the orientation is neither of the two, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_slider_set_value (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float value)"

.PP
Moves a slider to a value\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_slider_create\&. 
.br
\fIvalue\fP The new value, clamped into the range\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a slider\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "float schultz_slider_value (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns a slider's value\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_slider_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The value, or 0 when the node is not a slider\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_slider_set_step (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float step)"

.PP
Sets how far one arrow key moves a slider\&. The default is a hundredth of the range\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_slider_create\&. 
.br
\fIstep\fP The step\&. Must be greater than zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a step of zero or less, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a slider\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_progress_bar_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, uint32_t orientation, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a progress bar: a track with a filled portion\&. It draws and nothing else: it takes no focus and handles no events\&. The track is \fCborder\&.color\fP and the filled part \fCbackground\fP\&.
.PP
A vertical bar fills from the bottom, the way a column fills\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIorientation\fP SCHULTZ_ORIENT_HORIZONTAL or _VERTICAL\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when the orientation is neither of the two, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_progress_bar_set_value (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float value)"

.PP
Sets how far along a progress bar is\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_progress_bar_create\&. 
.br
\fIvalue\fP 0 to 1, clamped\&. On an unmeasured bar this is where the marker sits rather than how full the bar is\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a progress bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "float schultz_progress_bar_value (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns a progress bar's value\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_progress_bar_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The value, or 0 when the node is not a progress bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_progress_bar_set_indeterminate (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t indeterminate)"

.PP
Switches a progress bar between measured and unmeasured\&. Nothing in the toolkit knows what time it is, so an unmeasured bar does not animate itself\&. It draws a marker covering a third of the track, positioned by the value, and the host moves it the same way it moves anything else that animates\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_progress_bar_create\&. 
.br
\fIindeterminate\fP Nonzero for a bar whose end is not known\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a progress bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_bar_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, uint32_t orientation, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a scroll bar: a track with a draggable thumb\&. A real widget rather than painting inside a scroll view, so it can be styled, focused and used on its own to scroll anything at all\&.
.PP
The thumb's length is the fraction of the content that shows, floored so it stays grabbable in a very long document\&. Dragging keeps the point that was grabbed under the pointer; pressing the track beyond the thumb pages towards the press\&. Arrows step, page keys page, home and end jump\&.
.PP
The track is drawn in \fCborder\&.color\fP and the thumb in \fCbackground\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIorientation\fP SCHULTZ_ORIENT_HORIZONTAL or _VERTICAL\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_bar_on_change (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_scroll_bar_changed_fn\fP changed, void * context)"

.PP
Asks to be told when a scroll bar moves\&. Called for every reason a bar moves: a drag, a page, a key, or an application setting the value\&. This is how a bar used on its own scrolls something, and it is how a scroll view keeps its content and its bars in step\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_bar_create\&. 
.br
\fIchanged\fP The callback, or NULL to stop being told\&. 
.br
\fIcontext\fP Passed to it, unchanged\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a scroll bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_bar_set_range (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float content, float viewport)"

.PP
Tells a scroll bar how much there is and how much of it shows\&. Both are in the same units as the value, which are the content's own\&. A viewport at least as large as the content leaves nowhere to scroll, and the bar clamps its value to match\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_bar_create\&. 
.br
\fIcontent\fP Total length of what is being scrolled\&. Not negative\&. 
.br
\fIviewport\fP Length of the part that shows\&. Not negative\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a negative length, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a scroll bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_bar_set_value (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float value)"

.PP
Moves a scroll bar to an offset\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_bar_create\&. 
.br
\fIvalue\fP The offset, clamped between zero and the maximum\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a scroll bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "float schultz_scroll_bar_value (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns a scroll bar's offset\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_bar_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The offset, or 0 when the node is not a scroll bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "float schultz_scroll_bar_maximum (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns the furthest a scroll bar can be scrolled\&. The content length less the viewport length, never below zero\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_bar_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The maximum offset, or 0 when the node is not a scroll bar\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_view_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a scroll view: a window onto content larger than itself\&. The view owns four nodes\&. A viewport clips and offsets, the content sits inside it and is what the application fills, and two scroll bars sit along the bottom and right edges, each shown only when there is something to scroll in its direction\&.
.PP
Scrolling shifts where the content is drawn and hit tested rather than moving its bounds, so it costs a repaint and never a relayout, whatever the content is\&. Everything that scrolls moves the bars and the bars move the content, so the two can never disagree\&.
.PP
Three things move it\&. A mouse wheel, a bar dragged with either a cursor or a finger, and a finger dragged across the content itself: the content follows the finger, and a flick carries on and slows to a stop after the finger lifts\&. A cursor deliberately does not drag the content, because it has a wheel and a bar already and dragging is how a cursor selects text\&.
.PP
A finger that starts on something inside the view presses it if it stays still and scrolls if it moves, and the press is not also delivered: see schultz_events_mouse_button\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBschultz_handle\fP schultz_scroll_view_content (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns the node an application fills with the scrolled content\&. Give it a pane and children, or a size hint, and the view scrolls it\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_HANDLE_NONE\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The content node, or SCHULTZ_HANDLE_NONE when the node is not a scroll view\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBschultz_handle\fP schultz_scroll_view_bar (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t orientation)"

.PP
Returns one of a scroll view's two bars, to style or query it\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_HANDLE_NONE\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.br
\fIorientation\fP SCHULTZ_ORIENT_HORIZONTAL or _VERTICAL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The bar, or SCHULTZ_HANDLE_NONE when the node is not a scroll view\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_view_reveal (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_handle\fP target)"

.PP
Scrolls the least distance that brings a node into view\&. Does nothing when the node is already inside, which is the usual answer while walking a selection with the arrow keys\&. A node larger than the viewport is shown from its near edge rather than its far one\&.
.PP
The target may be any node under the view, not only a direct child of its content, so a row built out of several nodes can name whichever one should be brought into view\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.br
\fItarget\fP The node to bring into view\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a scroll view or the target is not live\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_view_set_bars (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t shown)"

.PP
Says whether this view may show its bars\&. Bars show by default, when the content is larger than the view\&. Turning them off leaves the view scrolling exactly as it did -- a finger still drags the content, a wheel still turns it -- and gives the strip of space a bar was taking back to the content\&. That is what a view on a touch panel wants, where nobody aims at a bar and every pixel of height is spoken for\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.br
\fIshown\fP Nonzero to let the bars appear, zero to keep them away\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a scroll view\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_view_set_drag_scrolls (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t drags)"

.PP
Says whether a pointer that is not a finger may drag the content\&. A finger always drags\&. A cursor does not, because it has a wheel and a bar already and dragging with one is how text is selected -- so turning this on is for a view holding nothing selectable, where dragging is the whole gesture\&. The keyboard's field of emoji is the case it exists for\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.br
\fIdrags\fP Nonzero to let any pointer drag the content\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a scroll view\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_view_drag_scrolls (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns whether any pointer may drag this view's content\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Nonzero when a cursor drags it as a finger does\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_view_shows_bars (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns whether this view may show its bars\&. Named for the question rather than as the mirror of the setter, because schultz_scroll_view_bar already means one of the two bar nodes and one word cannot mean both\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Nonzero when a bar appears once the content needs one\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_scroll_view_scroll_to (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_point\fP offset)"

.PP
Scrolls a view to an offset\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_scroll_view_create\&. 
.br
\fIoffset\fP How far into the content to scroll, clamped on both axes\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a scroll view\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_field_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, const char * text, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a text field: one line of editable text\&. The field edits itself\&. Typing inserts, backspace and delete remove, the arrows move by a character and with control by a word, home and end go to the ends, and shift with any of those extends the selection\&. Control with A, C, X and V select all, copy, cut and paste, and control with Z takes an edit back, with shift to put it forward again\&. A run of typing is one step of that history rather than one step per keystroke\&.
.PP
Cut, copy and paste need a clipboard; install one with schultz_tree_set_clipboard\&. Without one, everything else still works\&.
.PP
Enter is left alone, so a host can treat it as accepting the form\&.
.PP
The text scrolls sideways to keep the caret in view\&. Selection is drawn in \fCselection\&.color\fP, the text and caret in \fCtext\&.color\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fItext\fP Starting text, copied\&. NULL means empty\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_field_set_input_type (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t type)"

.PP
Says what kind of text this field holds\&. What it changes is the keyboard: a number field opens on the numbers, and neither a number nor a password field offers emoji however the window is set\&. It does not restrict what may be typed; a field that must hold only digits is a number field\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the field\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_text_field_create or one of its kin\&. 
.br
\fItype\fP One of the SCHULTZ_INPUT_* values\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a type that is not one of them\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_text_field_input_type (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns what kind of text a field holds\&. A masked field answers SCHULTZ_INPUT_PASSWORD whether or not it was told to, since that is what masking means\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the field\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_text_field_create or one of its kin\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
One of the SCHULTZ_INPUT_* values\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_field_set_mask (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t codepoint)"

.PP
Draws a mask character in place of each character a field holds\&. Everything else about the field is unchanged, so every text field accessor works on a masked one\&. What is masked is the drawing only: the field still holds the real text and schultz_text_get still returns it\&.
.PP
\fBCut and copy are refused while a mask is set\fP, so the text never reaches the clipboard\&. Cut still removes the selection\&. Paste still works\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_text_field_create\&. 
.br
\fIcodepoint\fP The character to draw, or 0 to show the text itself\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when asked to mask a text area\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_text_field_mask (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns the mask character a field is drawing, or 0 for none\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_text_field_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The codepoint, or 0\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_password_field_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, const char * text, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a password field: a text field with masking already on\&. The same widget as a text field, so it is created, read and written with the text field calls\&. Turning the mask off with schultz_text_field_set_mask turns it back into a plain field\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fItext\fP Starting text, copied\&. NULL means empty\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_area_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, const char * text, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a text area: wrapped, scrolling, editable text\&. Everything a text field does, plus wrapping at its own width, up and down between lines keeping the column, home and end on the current line, and enter inserting a line break\&.
.PP
It is three lines tall and its text scrolls inside it, keeping the caret in view\&. A box that got taller with every line typed would push whatever is under it down the screen, so growing is something to ask for rather than the default: see schultz_text_area_set_grows\&. Change the height with schultz_text_area_set_visible_lines\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fItext\fP Starting text, copied\&. NULL means empty\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const char * schultz_text_get (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns what a text field or text area holds\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A text field or text area node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The text, owned by the widget, or NULL when the node is neither\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_set (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const char * text)"

.PP
Replaces what a text field or text area holds\&. The replacement can be taken back like any other edit\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A text field or text area node\&. 
.br
\fItext\fP The new text, copied\&. NULL means empty\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is neither, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_field_set_spans (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const \fBschultz_span\fP * spans, uint32_t count)"

.PP
Says which stretches of a field's or an area's text are not set the way its style says\&. The same spans a Label takes, on text that can be edited\&. Everything moves with the words as they are typed, pasted and deleted: a stretch marked bold stays on the same characters, grows when something is typed inside it, and goes when the characters it marked go\&.
.PP
The list is copied, link strings and all\&. Spans covering nothing, or bytes past the end of the text, are dropped\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_text_field_create or _text_area_create\&. 
.br
\fIspans\fP The spans\&. May be NULL when count is zero\&. 
.br
\fIcount\fP How many spans\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node holds no editable text, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for NULL spans with a nonzero count, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_span\fP * schultz_text_field_spans (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t * out_count)"

.PP
The spans an editable text widget is carrying\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node holding editable text\&. 
.br
\fIout_count\fP Receives how many there are, or NULL if not wanted\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The spans, owned by the widget and valid until the text or the spans change, or NULL when there are none\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_field_set_span (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t from, uint32_t to, const \fBschultz_span\fP * look)"

.PP
Gives one stretch of the text a look of its own\&. What a Bold button does to a selection\&. A span the range lands in the middle of is split, and neighbours that end up saying the same thing are merged, so the list stays as short as what it describes\&.
.PP
Pass NULL for \fClook\fP to strip the range back to the widget's own style\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node holding editable text\&. 
.br
\fIfrom\fP First byte\&. 
.br
\fIto\fP One past the last byte\&. Cut to the end of the text\&. 
.br
\fIlook\fP What that stretch should look like, or NULL for plain\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node holds no editable text, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_field_set_typing (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const \fBschultz_span\fP * look)"

.PP
Says what the next thing typed should look like\&. What a Bold button does with nothing selected: there is no text to mark yet, so the look is remembered and given to whatever is typed next\&.
.PP
Without this, typing takes after the character before the caret, which is what continues a bold word when more is added to the end of it\&.
.PP
Moving the caret forgets it, because a look asked for in one place was not asked for in another\&. Pass NULL to forget it now\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node holding editable text\&. 
.br
\fIlook\fP What to type in, or NULL to go back to taking after the text\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node holds no editable text, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_area_set_visible_lines (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t lines)"

.PP
Sets how many lines tall a text area is\&. Three by default\&. A size hint on the node overrules it\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_text_area_create\&. 
.br
\fIlines\fP How many lines\&. Must be at least one\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for zero lines, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a text area\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_area_set_grows (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t grows)"

.PP
Lets a text area's height follow its text instead of scrolling\&. Off by default, because a box that gets taller with every line typed pushes whatever is under it down the screen\&. Turn it on for the cases that want it: a message composer that opens up as it is filled, or an area inside a scroll view, where the view supplies the bars and does the scrolling\&.
.PP
A growing area never shrinks below the height it asked for\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_text_area_create\&. 
.br
\fIgrows\fP Nonzero to follow the text\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a text area\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_set_selection (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t anchor, uint32_t caret)"

.PP
Sets the selection, as two byte offsets\&. The anchor is the end that stays put and the caret is the end that moves, so passing the same value for both places the caret and selects nothing\&. Both are clamped to the text's length\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A text field or text area node\&. 
.br
\fIanchor\fP Byte offset of the fixed end\&. 
.br
\fIcaret\fP Byte offset of the moving end\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is neither\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_text_selection (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t * out_anchor, uint32_t * out_caret)"

.PP
Reads the selection\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A text field or text area node\&. 
.br
\fIout_anchor\fP Receives the fixed end, or NULL if not wanted\&. 
.br
\fIout_caret\fP Receives the moving end, or NULL if not wanted\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is neither\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a canvas: a widget holding drawing the application records\&. The drawing is retained, not asked for each frame\&. Record into it when the data changes and it is replayed for free thereafter, which is what a chart wants: it redraws when its numbers change, not sixty times a second\&. From a scripting language above, that means a burst of calls when something happens and none at all in between\&.
.PP
Coordinates are the canvas's own, with the origin at its top left, and the drawing is clipped to its bounds\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_set_pixel_exact (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t exact)"

.PP
Draws this canvas in screen pixels rather than toolkit units\&. The toolkit usually draws everything larger on a screen that packs more pixels into the same space, so a line one wide covers three of them on a dense phone\&. That is right for a button and wrong for a drawing: a chart that wants a one pixel rule, or a picture meant to land on exact pixels, needs to reach the screen as it is\&.
.PP
With this set, one unit inside this canvas is one pixel of the screen\&. The canvas itself is still placed and sized like any other widget, so a canvas a hundred units wide still occupies a hundred units of the layout; what changes is that its own drawing space is then three hundred wide on a three times screen\&. Ask schultz_canvas_pixel_size for that number rather than working it out\&.
.PP
Nothing changes on a screen with nothing to scale, where a unit is already a pixel, or when the frame was told not to scale to the screen\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the canvas is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_canvas_create\&. 
.br
\fIexact\fP Nonzero to draw in screen pixels, zero for toolkit units\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_pixel_size (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t * out_width, uint32_t * out_height)"

.PP
The size of a canvas's drawing space in screen pixels\&. The same as its bounds on a screen with nothing to scale, and three times them on a dense phone\&. This is the number to draw against when the canvas is set to screen pixels, and the resolution a picture should be prepared at either way\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_canvas_create\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width in pixels\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height in pixels\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL pointer, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_begin (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Starts recording, clearing whatever was there\&. Every drawing call between this and schultz_canvas_end is kept\&. Calls made outside that pair are refused, so a half finished recording cannot end up on screen\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_canvas_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a canvas\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_end (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Finishes recording and marks the canvas for repaint\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node created by schultz_canvas_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a canvas, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY when the recording ran out of room part way through\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_group_begin (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float opacity, \fBschultz_shadow\fP shadow)"

.PP
Starts a group: everything until the end becomes one picture\&. The reason to want one is that a drawing made of several pieces is often one thing to the eye\&. A card is a rounded rectangle, a title and a button glyph, and it should cast \fBone\fP shadow and fade as \fBone\fP picture\&. Without a group it casts three shadows that overlap, and fading the three separately makes every place they overlap darker than it should be, because the same pixel is blended twice\&.
.PP
Groups nest, so a group may hold other groups, each with its own fading and its own shadow\&.
.PP
\fBWhy there is an end call\&.\fP A canvas is a stream of drawing calls rather than a bag of objects, so which pieces belong to the group can only be said as a range: everything between the two\&. And because groups nest, a start inside a group means a group inside that group, so only the end can say which one has finished\&. The end is also the moment the picture exists, which is when the shadow can be worked out at all: a shadow is cast by a finished shape\&.
.PP
A group left open when schultz_canvas_end is reached is closed there, so a drawing that returned early still appears rather than being thrown away\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIopacity\fP 0 for invisible, 1 for solid\&. 
.br
\fIshadow\fP The shadow the group casts, or schultz_shadow_none\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a canvas or is not recording, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_group_end (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Finishes the group started by schultz_canvas_group_begin\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a canvas or is not recording, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when no group is open, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_fill_rect (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_rect\fP rect, \fBschultz_paint\fP paint, float radius)"

.PP
Records a filled rectangle, rounded when a radius is given\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIrect\fP The rectangle, in the canvas's own coordinates\&. 
.br
\fIpaint\fP What to fill with\&. 
.br
\fIradius\fP Corner radius, or zero for square corners\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a canvas or is not recording, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_stroke_rect (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_rect\fP rect, \fBschultz_stroke\fP stroke, float radius)"

.PP
Records a rectangle outline\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIrect\fP The rectangle, in the canvas's own coordinates\&. 
.br
\fIstroke\fP What the outline is drawn with\&. 
.br
\fIradius\fP Corner radius, or zero for square corners\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_stroke_polygon (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const \fBschultz_point\fP * points, uint32_t count, \fBschultz_stroke\fP stroke, int32_t closed)"

.PP
Records a polygon outline, or a line through a series of points\&. The counterpart to schultz_canvas_fill_polygon\&. Drawing the segments one line at a time is not the same thing: each line would end square at every corner, and a thick outline shows it\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIpoints\fP The vertices, in order\&. Copied\&. 
.br
\fIcount\fP How many\&. Must be at least two\&. 
.br
\fIstroke\fP What the outline is drawn with\&. 
.br
\fIclosed\fP Nonzero joins the last point back to the first, making a closed outline; zero leaves it open, making a polyline\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_measure_text (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP font, const char * utf8, \fBschultz_size\fP * out_size)"

.PP
Measures a string, without drawing it\&. Text can be drawn onto a canvas, and laying anything out around it needs its size\&. The toolkit shapes its own text, so this is the width the string will actually take rather than an estimate\&.
.PP
This does not record anything and does not need a canvas that is open\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP A tree with a font system\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIfont\fP A loaded font\&. 
.br
\fIutf8\fP The text, NUL terminated\&. 
.br
\fIout_size\fP Receives the width and the line height\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_stroke_ellipse (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_rect\fP rect, \fBschultz_stroke\fP stroke)"

.PP
Records an ellipse outline inscribed in a rectangle\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIrect\fP The rectangle the ellipse is drawn inside\&. 
.br
\fIstroke\fP What the outline is drawn with\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_image (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_handle\fP image, \fBschultz_rect\fP source, \fBschultz_rect\fP dest, uint8_t opacity)"

.PP
Records a picture, scaled from a part of it into a rectangle\&. The source rectangle picks a region of the image out, which is what a sprite sheet or an atlas wants\&. To draw the whole picture, pass a source rectangle covering it; a source with no width or height means the whole image\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIimage\fP A loaded image, from schultz_image_load_file\&. 
.br
\fIsource\fP The part of the image to take, in image pixels\&. 
.br
\fIdest\fP Where to put it, in the canvas's own coordinates\&. 
.br
\fIopacity\fP 0 for invisible, 255 for opaque\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_clip_begin (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_rect\fP rect)"

.PP
Narrows what the drawing after it may touch\&. Pairs with schultz_canvas_clip_end\&. Clips nest: each one narrows what the one before it allowed, and never widens it\&.
.PP
The node's own bounds already clip a canvas, so this is for masking part of a drawing rather than for keeping it inside its widget\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIrect\fP What to keep, in the canvas's own coordinates\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_clip_end (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Restores the clip in force before the matching push\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_offset_begin (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float dx, float dy)"

.PP
Shifts everything drawn after it\&. Pairs with schultz_canvas_offset_end, and they nest: an offset inside another adds to it\&. Drawing a repeated shape at several places is what this is for, without adding the position into every coordinate\&.
.PP
This is a shift and nothing more\&. To turn what is drawn, use schultz_canvas_rotation_begin, which nests with this one: an offset pushed inside a rotation moves along the turned axes\&. There is no scale, because schultz_canvas_set_pixel_exact already says what a unit is worth\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIdx\fP How far to move along x\&. 
.br
\fIdy\fP How far to move along y\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_offset_end (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Restores the offset in force before the matching push\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_rotation_begin (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float degrees, float cx, float cy)"

.PP
Turns everything drawn after it\&. Pairs with schultz_canvas_rotation_end, and they nest: a rotation inside another adds to it, and an offset pushed inside a rotation moves along the turned axes\&.
.PP
The angle is in \fBdegrees\fP, clockwise, because y grows downward\&. The centre is in the canvas's own coordinates, so turning a drawing about its own middle is one call rather than an offset either side of it\&.
.PP
This turns the drawing, not the canvas\&. The node keeps its upright bounds, takes the same space in the layout, clips to the same rectangle and is hit tested the same\&. Nothing turned can escape the canvas\&.
.PP
Shapes, images and text all turn\&. Text at an angle is drawn from the font's outlines rather than from the cached upright pictures of each letter, so it stays sharp, and it gives up hinting the way rotated text does in every toolkit\&. An emoji has no outline to draw from and is turned as the picture it is\&.
.PP
A clip pushed inside a rotation is the upright box around the turned rectangle rather than the turned rectangle itself, so it lets through a little more than it names\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.br
\fIdegrees\fP How far to turn, clockwise\&. 
.br
\fIcx\fP The centre to turn about, along x\&. 
.br
\fIcy\fP The centre to turn about, along y\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_canvas_rotation_end (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Restores the rotation in force before the matching push\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A canvas that is recording\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or an error as above\&. 
.RE
.PP

